WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:21.147
One while Fritz and I were out exploring, I noticed some interesting bushes.

2
00:00:22.087 --> 00:00:25.409
They were loaded with small, white berries that were waxy in appearance.

3
00:00:26.369 --> 00:00:30.871
"You know, Fritz, we might be able to use these berries to make candles," I said.

4
00:00:30.891 --> 00:00:33.232
"Oh, that would make Mother happy.

5
00:00:33.972 --> 00:00:37.434
She doesn't like having to set aside her work and go to bed as soon as the sun

6
00:00:37.494 --> 00:00:43.118
sets." The wax would be more brittle than beeswax, but it would burn well and with a pleasant

7
00:00:43.178 --> 00:00:49.061
scent. Having the donkey with us, we quickly gathered enough berries to fill one of the large canvas

8
00:00:49.121 --> 00:00:49.942
sacks he carried.

9
00:00:50.742 --> 00:00:52.263
We then continued on our way.

10
00:00:53.164 --> 00:00:56.165
Very soon we came upon a tree with a whole colony of birds.

11
00:00:57.046 --> 00:01:00.228
Their nests were built in a mass around the tree trunk and branches.

12
00:01:01.172 --> 00:01:06.015
A roof-like covering of straw and grass protected the colony from the rain and hot sun.

13
00:01:06.035 --> 00:01:08.396
"The nests are like rooms in a house!"

14
00:01:08.696 --> 00:01:09.196
said Fritz.

15
00:01:10.097 --> 00:01:13.359
We watched as the birds swarmed in and out by the thousands.

16
00:01:14.639 --> 00:01:19.622
Most of them were plain brown birds, but there were also a few beautiful, bright parakeets.

17
00:01:20.523 --> 00:01:23.544
Fritz was curious about the nests so he climbed the tree.

18
00:01:24.285 --> 00:01:28.067
He put his hands into several nests, hoping to capture a young bird.

19
00:01:28.087 --> 00:01:29.928
"Ow!" he cried.

20
00:01:30.444 --> 00:01:31.645
"Something pecked my finger."

21
00:01:32.446 --> 00:01:34.748
He reached back in and pulled out a small bird.

22
00:01:35.708 --> 00:01:39.772
Fritz's prize was not one of the brown birds, but a pretty green parakeet.

23
00:01:40.572 --> 00:01:42.994
For the moment he put it in his pocket and we walked on.

24
00:01:44.035 --> 00:01:48.138
Our next discovery was a strange tree with bark that oozed a gummy resin.

25
00:01:48.959 --> 00:01:50.180
Fritz scraped some off.

26
00:01:51.081 --> 00:01:51.901
"Look at this, Father.

27
00:01:52.222 --> 00:01:54.603
When I stretch this gum, it springs right back.

28
00:01:55.284 --> 00:01:56.685
Do you think it could be rubber?"

29
00:01:56.705 --> 00:02:00.108
"If it is, that's a most valuable discovery."

30
00:02:00.832 --> 00:02:01.712
"Why is it valuable?

31
00:02:02.033 --> 00:02:04.594
I've only seen rubber used for erasing pencil marks.

32
00:02:05.394 --> 00:02:06.354
How else can you use it?"

33
00:02:07.175 --> 00:02:09.336
"I would try to make waterproof boots and shoes!"

34
00:02:10.196 --> 00:02:13.197
Pleased with our discoveries, we headed back to Falconhurst.

35
00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:23.041
The next morning the boys wanted to make candles as soon as breakfast was over.

36
00:02:23.781 --> 00:02:27.003
We put the berries in a large, shallow iron pot on the fire.

37
00:02:27.783 --> 00:02:29.864
The sweet-scented wax melted rapidly.

38
00:02:30.592 --> 00:02:34.194
We skimmed it from the surface of the berry juice, and put it in another pot.

39
00:02:34.874 --> 00:02:37.935
I then took the wicks my wife had prepared and dipped them in the wax.

40
00:02:38.855 --> 00:02:43.557
"These are very skinny candles," said Ernest as the boys hung them on a bush to dry.

41
00:02:43.577 --> 00:02:45.798
"They're not done yet," I said.

42
00:02:46.138 --> 00:02:47.879
"We're going to dip them several times."

43
00:02:48.759 --> 00:02:52.601
We soon had sturdy candles, which we left in a cool spot to harden.

44
00:02:53.401 --> 00:02:56.863
And that night Falconhurst was brilliantly illuminated for the first time.

45
00:02:57.703 --> 00:03:00.929
"You are very clever indeed," my wife said to me.

46
00:03:00.949 --> 00:03:04.412
"I just wish you could figure out a way to make butter.

47
00:03:05.213 --> 00:03:09.396
Day after day I'm annoyed about the large amount of good cream that goes bad before I can

48
00:03:09.536 --> 00:03:09.916
use it."

49
00:03:09.936 --> 00:03:11.698
"Let me think about that.

50
00:03:12.038 --> 00:03:14.780
I hoped we would find a churn on the wreck, but we didn't."

51
00:03:16.542 --> 00:03:18.904
By the next morning, I had come up with a plan.

52
00:03:18.924 --> 00:03:22.547
"Bring me one of our gourd bottles," I said to Jack.

53
00:03:23.328 --> 00:03:25.549
He quickly fetched a gourd that we had hollowed out.

54
00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:28.392
We had also added a small hole at one end.

55
00:03:29.224 --> 00:03:33.427
I partially filled the gourd with cream and then tightly plugged the hole with a piece of cork.

56
00:03:33.447 --> 00:03:35.528
"Here, boys," I said.

57
00:03:36.228 --> 00:03:39.610
"You can continue the job while I become a carpenter and build a cart."

58
00:03:40.391 --> 00:03:42.592
I gave them some directions and set about my own work.

59
00:03:43.592 --> 00:03:45.734
The boys pounded four stakes into the ground.

60
00:03:46.834 --> 00:03:49.796
Then they fastened a square of sailcloth to the stakes with cord.

61
00:03:50.757 --> 00:03:53.758
After that they placed the gourd full of cream on this cloth cradle.

62
00:03:54.719 --> 00:03:59.189
Each taking a side of the cloth, they rolled the gourd back and forth without stopping.

63
00:03:59.209 --> 00:04:02.230
"This is hard work," complained Ernest.

64
00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:04.610
"No it isn't," said Jack.

65
00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:05.891
"This is fun."

66
00:04:05.911 --> 00:04:09.312
"Keep at it, boys," I said as I looked up from my work.

67
00:04:09.332 --> 00:04:11.332
"This is going to take a while."

68
00:04:12.173 --> 00:04:14.113
I expected little Franz to complain too.

69
00:04:14.893 --> 00:04:18.735
But he was just happy to be helping his older brothers with some real and useful work.

70
00:04:19.735 --> 00:04:23.036
After half an hour I told the boys to stop and look inside the gourd.

71
00:04:23.724 --> 00:04:25.305
"What's happened to the cream?"

72
00:04:25.706 --> 00:04:27.667
asked little Franz, shaking the gourd.

73
00:04:27.687 --> 00:04:29.628
"We've turned it into butter!"

74
00:04:30.029 --> 00:04:30.569
said Jack.

75
00:04:30.589 --> 00:04:34.272
"Now take the contents to your mother with my compliments," I said.

76
00:04:34.972 --> 00:04:37.694
My wife was delighted with the results from my gourd "churn."

77
00:04:38.455 --> 00:04:41.277
That night at supper we had plenty of butter to go with our meal.

78
00:04:41.297 --> 00:04:45.039
"You've more than made up for scaring me with that sailboat!"

79
00:04:45.379 --> 00:04:45.780
she said.
